{"agent":"dedupe-t2-engine","data":{"budget":"","claim":"An exact TF-IDF cosine over an inverted index stored in index.db (postings packed per term; df = postings length) is both faster and exact; the FTS5 shortlist changes the top-3 above the show floor for ~5% of queries, so 'same results without FTS5' cannot hold for it. The engine uses no FTS5.","falsifier":"Shortlist disagreement 0/300, or exact-index p95 above 50 ms at 6k records.","item":"B-similar-engine","mechanism":"BM25 and TF-IDF cosine rank differently; a record with a high cosine can fall outside BM25's top 50 of its kind. FTS5 also scores every matching row before LIMIT, so the shortlist saves little. Packed postings make a query read only its own terms' rows.","probe":"scratchpad eng/proto.py, proto2.py, lat.py on run_nemo_run's log (6,201-6,248 records, read-only copy)","probe_output":"FTS5 pool50/kind + rerank: median 14.7 ms, p95 44.8 ms; top-3>=0.35 differs from exact on 16/300 queries. SQL row postings + Python: p95 27 ms. Packed-blob postings: median 0.8 ms, p95 5.6 ms. Final engine: warm store query median 1.8 / p95 9.5 ms; cold open+query (per add) median 13.8 / p95 31.6 ms; in memory p95 8.0 ms; store vs memory identical result lists 300/300. Cost: Store.rebuild on rnr 0.46-0.54 s -> 0.98 s (median of 7) for tokenizing and weighing 6.2k records. Fixture: engine dup_r3 0.976 rel_r5 0.957 lenient_p 0.824 hard_above 0.15 dup_asked 0.488 (reference 0.951/0.957/0.824/0.15/0.463); on 5 random half splits (duplicate clusters kept whole) the engine is >= the reference on every half.","question":"Does the FTS5 top-50-per-kind shortlist + TF-IDF rerank give the same answers as an exact TF-IDF cosine, and what does each cost per add at 6k records?","sources":["research R-dedupe-matchers; tests/test_dedupe_eval.py; /home/delian/src/run_nemo_run/.ddflow/events"],"verdict":"CONFIRMED"},"id":"e16f0e1798210f01be02d27ef","kind":"research.recorded","lamport":7618,"schema":1,"subject":"R-similar-exact","ts":"2